"," grant"," *","admin","*"," if"," the"," process"," token"," isn","'t"," elevated","."," Dele","ting"," Windows",".","old"," non","-e","lev","ated",":"," ACL"," on"," Windows",".","old"," —"," typically"," grants"," \"","SY","STEM","\""," and"," \"","Administ","rators","\""," full"," control",";"," the"," folder"," itself"," usually"," inher","its"," from"," C",":\\"," allowing"," Users"," read","/","execute"," only","."," So"," non","-admin"," cannot"," delete","."," Also"," the"," sand","box"," danger","-full","-access"," probably"," just"," removes"," file"," restrictions"," within"," same"," token",".\n\n","So"," realistically"," achievable"," without"," admin",":\n","-"," ","用户"," Temp"," ","清理"," (~","1",".","3","GB","):"," own"," files",","," delet","able"," by"," user","."," Sand","box"," may"," allow"," since"," it","'s"," a"," platform"," temp"," area"," (","env"," TEM","P"," points"," there",")."," \n","-"," Browser"," c","aches",","," IN","et","Cache",":"," own"," profile",","," fine",".\n","-"," Re","cycle"," bin",":"," own",","," currently"," empty"," (","0","MB"," measured",","," though"," measuring"," may"," have"," been"," blocked","..."," it"," reported"," ","0",".","0","MB"," \"","存在","\"",").\n","-"," C",":\\","M","113","0","M","FP","..."," folder"," (","HP"," driver","):"," check"," ACL",";"," may"," be"," delet","able"," if"," ACL"," grants"," Users"," modify","."," Created"," by"," installer"," possibly"," under"," admin","."," We"," can"," check",".\n","-"," One","Drive","Temp"," etc",".\n\n","Admin","-needed"," (","should"," tell"," user"," to"," run"," as"," admin"," or"," provide"," a"," script"," they"," run"," elevated","):\n","-"," Windows",".","old"," ","42",".","6","GB"," —"," needs"," admin","."," Provide"," instructions","/","script",":"," e",".g",".,"," clean","m","gr"," or"," disk"," cleanup",","," or"," `","take","own","`","/","`","ic","ac","ls","`"," then"," Remove","-","Item",","," or"," DIS","M","."," Best",":"," use"," \"","磁盘","清理","\""," or"," cmd",":"," `","rd"," /","s"," /","q"," C",":\\","Windows",".","old","`"," after"," taking"," ownership","."," Or"," Settings"," >"," System"," >"," Storage"," >"," Temporary"," files"," >"," Previous"," Windows"," installation",".\n","-"," hiber","fil",".s","ys"," ","22",".","3","GB"," —"," power","cfg"," /","h"," off"," (","admin",")."," Ask"," user"," if"," they"," use"," h","ibernate","."," Many"," desktop"," users"," don","'t",";"," but"," sleep"," might"," use"," it","?"," Hybrid"," sleep"," uses"," hiber","fil","."," If"," they"," use"," ","快速","启动"," (","Fast"," Startup","),"," disabling"," hiber","nation"," dis","ables"," fast"," startup"," too","."," Trade","-off"," to"," present",".\n","-"," Software","Distribution"," cache"," small",".\n\n","Before"," proposing",","," wait"," for"," the"," background"," measurements"," (","pw","sh","-","1",":"," leftover"," folders",";"," pw","sh","-","2",":"," user"," profile"," dir","s",")."," Let"," me"," collect"," them","."," Also"," re","-","verify"," Windows",".","old"," existence"," (","maybe"," it","'s"," real",";"," Get","-","Item"," not","-found"," is"," sand","box"," artifact",")."," Also",","," maybe"," I"," should"," check"," whether"," Windows",".","old"," really"," is"," a"," full"," copy"," —"," list"," its"," top"," entries"," to"," confirm"," it","'s"," the"," old"," Windows"," install",".\n\n","Let"," me"," collect"," job"," outputs"," and"," run"," verification"," commands","."]},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934613240,"chunk":{"type":"block-start","index":1,"blockType":"text"}},{"type":"text-chunks","time0":1788934613240,"index":1,"dt":[24,0,1,45,0,0,0,1,56,0,66,17,0,1,3,0,6,10,0,8,8,0,0,0,0,8,1,0,0,0,12,0,0,26,0,21,0,23,0,0,19,1,0,21,24,38,1],"texts":["两个","测量","任务","在","后台","跑","，","先","收集","结果","，","同时","核实","一下","刚才","的","异常","发现","（","`","Get","-","Item","`"," ","说"," Windows",".","old"," ","不存在","，","但","枚举","显示","存在","且","高达"," ","42","GB","，","需要","确认","不是","误","报","）："]},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934613732,"chunk":{"type":"block-start","index":2,"blockType":"tool-call"}},{"type":"tool-call-chunks","time0":1788934613732,"index":2,"dt":[11,1,0,0,13,0,0,1,19,0,0,0,43,0,0,0,26,0,47,1,0,0,0,0,44,0,0,10],"id":"call_00_oYZpmYHiVpzu2iw2BR6a9146","name":"job_output","args":["","{","\"","job","_id","\"",": ","\"","pw","sh","-","1","\"",", ","\"","wait","\"",": ","true",", ","\"","time","out","_ms","\"",": ","120","000","}"]},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934613979,"chunk":{"type":"block-start","index":3,"blockType":"tool-call"}},{"type":"tool-call-chunks","time0":1788934613979,"index":3,"dt":[25,0,0,21,0,0,1,23,1,0,0,0,46,0,0,0,1,20,16,1,0,32,0,0,0,25,17,33],"id":"call_01_uw2ZqMobcHx1v7uyGLea5353","name":"job_output","args":["","{","\"","job","_id","\"",": ","\"","pw","sh","-","2","\"",", ","\"","wait","\"",": ","true",", ","\"","time","out","_ms","\"",": ","120","000","}"]},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934614304,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting! `C:\\Windows.old` — earlier the recursive enumeration returned 43.6 GB, but Get-Item says it doesn't exist. That's very strange. Wait... the sandbox! The workspace-write sandbox gives a *virtualized/filtered view* of the filesystem? Earlier Get-PSDrive showed all zeros, and Get-Volume/WMI denied. But then .NET DriveInfo worked and showed real numbers.\n\nHmm wait, maybe the sandbox presents a *redirected* C:\\Windows.old? No... Actually maybe earlier the directory listing of C:\\ root showed \"Windows.old\" but that may be a sandbox artifact showing a fake empty dir? No — the recursive size calc returned 43,637.7 MB. Odd.\n\nActually wait — maybe Test-Path earlier: '旧系统Windows.old 存在 大小: 43,637.7 MB'. Then the C:\\ root listing shows Windows.old dir. But now Get-Item C:\\Windows.old fails \"does not exist\". Very inconsistent. Unless... the sandbox file view differs between commands? Or Get-Item without -ErrorAction... it failed with \"Cannot find path\". Note earlier Test-Path returned true and Get-ChildItem -Recurse enumerated.\n\nPossibly: the sandbox denies enumeration under some paths in certain API calls? Hmm, Get-Item is Core, while Get-ChildItem might have succeeded because errors were silenced and it enumerated what it could... but it got 43.6GB from somewhere. That means it actually read real content.\n\nWait, maybe there's a subtlety: under workspace-write mode, the harness may *redirect* writes; for reads it's transparent. Get-Item \"C:\\Windows.old\" says not exist while Get-ChildItem C:\\ shows Windows.old and recursion measures 43.6GB — could the harness be returning *fake* directory listing with cached sizes? No, that's not plausible.\n\nAlternative explanation: These pwsh calls run under different sandbox modes? No, all workspace-write.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the issue: Get-Item requires the item to be visible without -Force? No, Windows.old is a normal hidden? Windows.old usually has Hidden+System attributes? Actually Windows.old is typically just a directory with normal attributes (sometimes hidden). Get-Item C:\\Windows.old should find it if it exists regardless of hidden.\n\nUnless the sandbox denies *metadata* access via Get-Item for paths outside workspace and reports NotFound, while Get-ChildItem traversal works via different API path that is permitted... messy. Let me verify by trying dir (cmd) and Get-ChildItem C:\\ -Force to re-list, plus check the top-level of Windows.old.\n\nfsutil dirty query errored with \"Access is denied\" (Error 5) - probably sandbox blocking fsutil? Actually the fsutil output shows \"Error 5: Access is denied.\" Hmm that was probably from fsutil. Or from Get-Item? The structure: Get-Item threw and the catch printed 'Windows.old 读取失败: ...' — no wait, the catch printed a message starting with \"Cannot find path...\" in stderr with exit code 1. Actually the stderr shows Get-Item error. Then the catch's string 'Windows.old 读取失败: Cannot find path...' — hmm, the stderr shows the raw Get-Item error text \"Get-Item : Cannot find path 'C:\\Windows.old' because it does not exist.\" but I don't see 'Windows.old 读取失败:' prefix. Odd; maybe formatting. And \"Error 5: Access is denied.\" was the fsutil output line (fsutil prints error 5 because it needs admin). So current user is NOT admin.\n\nNot admin — that's a big constraint. Cleaning C:\\Windows.old requires admin (take ownership). hiberfil.sys change requires admin. Deleting files in C:\\Windows, SoftwareDistribution etc requires admin. Even emptying recycle bin needs per-user rights (own bin is fine).\n\nBut wait — can we even delete anything on C:\\ given sandbox (workspace-write only allows modifying D:\\windoc\\desktop\\3 and platform temp)? The pwsh tool would be sandboxed; deleting under C:\\Users\\xiaowenbo\\AppData\\Local\\Temp might count as \"platform temporary area\" writable. Realistically, the big wins (Windows.old 42GB, hiberfil 22GB) need admin elevation anyway, which this sandboxed non-admin pwsh cannot do. Even if sandbox allowed, the process isn't elevated.\n\nHmm, but wait: does the DSH sandbox run pwsh as the normal user with full non-admin rights to the machine (just file-policy restricted), or in a restricted token? It's likely a normal token with file sandbox. Non-admin can still:\n- delete own user temp files\n- empty recycle bin\n- delete browser caches (own profile)\n- remove C:\\M1130MFP... if user has rights? C:\\ root folder creation by users is usually not permitted (needs admin to create folder in root, but the folder exists with ACLs possibly granting Users). Depends.\n\nLet me reconsider Windows.old. Something inconsistent about it. Let me test more directly:\n1. cmd /c dir C:\\Windows.old\n2. Get-ChildItem C:\\ -Force listing again to see Windows.old\n3. Try to compute size of C:\\Windows.old again.\n\nMaybe sandbox *virtualizes* C:\\Windows.old: i.e., when earlier command measured 43.6GB, it was actually reading real data... and Get-Item \"cannot find\" is bizarre.\n\nOR: The environment could be running commands on a *different* machine than the user's actual C:? No.\n\nWait — actually there's another possibility: these pwsh commands may execute inside a Windows Sandbox or a container VM where DSH provides a fake C:\\ listing... no, DriveInfo returned realistic sizes for a real 238GB SSD.\n\nActually! Earlier the temp env var showed C:\\Users\\XIAOWE~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\dsh-vl9AWx — that's the real user xiaowenbo.\n\nHmm, let me just re-run checks. Also earlier root listing DID show Windows.old directory plus DumpStack.log.tmp, hiberfil.sys 22799 MB, swapfile.sys 256MB. Wait, hiberfil.sys 22.3GB but earlier Test-Path said '不存在' for hiberfil.sys?! Test-Path -LiteralPath \"C:\\hiberfil.sys\" returned not exists in the targets loop, yet Get-ChildItem C:\\ -Force shows hiberfil.sys 22,799.3 MB. Contradiction again!\n\nPattern: Test-Path -LiteralPath and Get-Item -LiteralPath fail for root-level items (hiberfil.sys, Windows.old) while Get-ChildItem C:\\ -Force lists them fine and even allows recursion+sizing. This smells like the file sandbox: direct stat of disallowed path returns not-found, but directory enumeration is filtered in a way that includes... no, enumeration showed them and sizes measured real bytes.\n\nActually — hmm, maybe Get-ChildItem C:\\ -Force output for hiberfil.sys/Windows.old is *injected/simulated* by the sandbox to represent what exists but sizes could be... no, sizes were computed from Length property; hiberfil.sys Length 22799.3MB.\n\nAlternatively, the pwsh sandbox uses an API-redirection layer where some .NET file APIs (File.Exists / Get-Item provider path resolution) get denied/blocked and return false/not-found for protected root paths, while FileSystem enumeration APIs under the same host pass through? Honestly the mechanism doesn't matter much; what matters: which operations work.\n\nPriority: I should not burn too much time reverse-engineering the sandbox. Key question: what cleanup can actually be executed, and which parts need user consent/escalation.\n\nGiven approval policy \"ask\", I can escalate commands with sandbox_permissions (danger-full-access) when denied, which raises an approval prompt to the user. But even danger-full-access won't grant *admin* if the process token isn't elevated. Deleting Windows.old non-elevated: ACL on Windows.old — typically grants \"SYSTEM\" and \"Administrators\" full control; the folder itself usually inherits from C:\\ allowing Users read/execute only. So non-admin cannot delete. Also the sandbox danger-full-access probably just removes file restrictions within same token.\n\nSo realistically achievable without admin:\n- 用户 Temp 清理 (~1.3GB): own files, deletable by user. Sandbox may allow since it's a platform temp area (env TEMP points there). \n- Browser caches, INetCache: own profile, fine.\n- Recycle bin: own, currently empty (0MB measured, though measuring may have been blocked... it reported 0.0MB \"存在\").\n- C:\\M1130MFP... folder (HP driver): check ACL; may be deletable if ACL grants Users modify. Created by installer possibly under admin. We can check.\n- OneDriveTemp etc.\n\nAdmin-needed (should tell user to run as admin or provide a script they run elevated):\n- Windows.old 42.6GB — needs admin. Provide instructions/script: e.g., cleanmgr or disk cleanup, or `takeown`/`icacls` then Remove-Item, or DISM. Best: use \"磁盘清理\" or cmd: `rd /s /q C:\\Windows.old` after taking ownership. Or Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files > Previous Windows installation.\n- hiberfil.sys 22.3GB — powercfg /h off (admin). Ask user if they use hibernate. Many desktop users don't; but sleep might use it? Hybrid sleep uses hiberfil. If they use 快速启动 (Fast Startup), disabling hibernation disables fast startup too. Trade-off to present.\n- SoftwareDistribution cache small.\n\nBefore proposing, wait for the background measurements (pwsh-1: leftover folders; pwsh-2: user profile dirs). Let me collect them