{"type":"assistant/message","seq":85,"time":1788934746371,"data":{"turn":2,"step":3,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"Important changes:\n1. Approval policy changed from \"ask\" to \"never\"\n2. File policy changed from \"workspace-write\" to \"danger-full-access\" — the DSH file sandbox does not restrict file modifications now!\n\nSo the user has granted full file access. But approval prompts are disabled — meaning I cannot request escalation (sandbox_permissions is pointless now since we're already danger-full-access).\n\nFindings so far:\n- C:\\ root leftover folders:\n  - C:\\M1130MFP_M1210MFP_Full_Solution: 544.7 MB — HP printer driver installer leftovers, safe to delete if printer drivers already installed\n  - C:\\opt: 0 MB\n  - C:\\OneDriveTemp: 0 MB\n  - C:\\$SysReset: 181.5 MB — related to system reset (Windows recovery). Can contain logs. Sometimes needed for system reset. Generally can be cleaned after a while but it's system reset leftovers — better to leave or careful. Actually $SysReset holds data used for \"Reset this PC\" and update rollback? It can be cleaned by disk cleanup usually. Hmm. Actually C:\\$SysReset is used for system reset scenarios. Its content: ResetLog, etc. Cleaning is possible but it's protected and needs admin. Its size is only 181MB, low priority.\n  - C:\\inetpub: 0 MB (empty IIS folder)\n  - C:\\ProgramData: 2,749.5 MB (2.7GB) — contains app data; not wholesale-cleanable, but there may be specific caches. Maybe skip unless analyzing deeper.\n- User profile:\n  - AppData\\Local: 51 GB! Huge. Need to break down.\n  - Downloads: 284 MB\n  - Desktop: 0 MB (interesting — desktop measured 0.0 MB, maybe files are elsewhere or measurement issue since pwsh-2 still running)\n  - Pictures: 65.7 MB\n  - pwsh-2 still running (it'll also check Temp, INetCache, Chrome, Edge caches).\n\nThe huge item: user Temp 1.3GB earlier measured at AppData\\Local\\Temp — wait earlier we measured 用户Temp = 1,341.6 MB. And