a works for user).\n\nAlso check if Windows Update cleanup needed (small, 2.4MB).\n\nNote: even with danger-full-access file policy, the *process token* is still non-admin (xiaowenbo, not elevated). Deleting C:\\Windows.old requires admin privileges via ACL. Danger-full-access in DSH refers to the file sandbox of the harness; but does pwsh run elevated? Earlier WindowsIdentity said non-admin. Deleting Windows.old will hit Access Denied unless the folder ACL grants the user or the sandbox bypasses ACL (it shouldn't). We might need takeown/icacls which need admin. If truly non-elevated, Windows.old deletion may fail. Hmm. But wait — maybe the harness runs pwsh under an elevated service token? The WindowsIdentity check said Administrators role = False, so no.\n\nHmm, but that seems to make the biggest win impossible... unless DSH's danger-full-access uses a privileged file backend that performs operations as a different (privileged) process. The pwsh tool description says commands may run under a file sandbox; with danger-full-access \"The DSH file sandbox does not restrict file modifications by available operations.\" It's still the same pwsh token presumably.\n\nWe'll find out by attempting. But note approval policy \"never\": sandbox denials are final and I must not set sandbox_permissions. So if a deletion is ACL-denied, no escalation possible — explain to user to run as admin.\n\nLet me proceed with analysis. Steps:\n- collect pwsh-2\n- verify Windows.old & list top\n- AppData\\Local breakdown (background, could be slow; limit with shallow top-level dirs only: measure each direct child of AppData\\Local separately — but recursion of 50GB may take a while. Use background job.)\n\nLet me also check hibernation config."},{"type":"text","text":"权限已放开（full access）。现在把分析做完：确认 Windows.old 真实情况、AppData\\Local 里那 ~50GB 到底是什么、休眠配置如何："},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_IBCqVCXDsShlHcoIqQNf9848","name":"job_output","arguments":"{\"job_id\": \"pwsh-2\", \"wait\": true, \"time