 1.3GB earlier measured at AppData\\Local\\Temp — wait earlier we measured 用户Temp = 1,341.6 MB. And AppData\\Local total = 51GB. So AppData\\Local has ~50GB beyond temp! Something big inside — could be Docker, WSL, package caches, WeChat, browser profiles, etc. We should break down AppData\\Local.\n\nAlso verify Windows.old real size (43.6GB per earlier measurement). Now with danger-full-access, Get-Item may work. Let me re-check Windows.old, hiberfil (already seen 22.3GB), swapfile.\n\nGiven \"never\" approval, I must be careful: cleanup actions are destructive; no approval prompts possible. But user explicitly asked \"帮我清理下c盘空间\" twice, and granted full access + never-approve. That's clear intent to proceed. Still, for irreversible deletions of big folders (Windows.old is safe-ish - it's old Windows, standard to delete; hiberfil disabling changes feature behavior), I should present a plan and maybe ask via ask_user_question for the few judgment calls (hibernation). But user disabled approvals... ask_user_question tool is separate from sandbox approval; still usable. Actually with the change to \"never\", prompting still works via ask_user_question (it's a chat question, not an approval). It might be cleaner to ask about hibernation preference since that changes system behavior (loses hibernation + fast startup). For Windows.old — generally safe to delete and standard practice; but it's the user's only rollback path from the last feature update. Windows.old usually auto-deletes after 10 days. Its presence suggests a recent Windows update within 10 days. Deleting it frees ~42.6GB. That's the single biggest win and the main purpose.\n\nGiven the scale (Windows.old 42GB, hiberfil 22GB, temp 1.3GB, M1130 545MB, $SysReset 181MB), let me first finish analysis:\n1. Get pwsh-2 output (AppData breakdown continues + browser caches).\n2. Verify Windows.old with Get-Item now under full access + confirm it's the old Windows (list top-level).\n3. Break down AppData\\Local to find the ~50GB.\n4. Check hibernation status: powercfg /a (may need admin for some queries; powercfg /a works for user).\n\nAlso check if Windows Update cleanup needed (small, 2.4MB).\n\nNote: even with danger-full-access file policy, the *process token* is still non-admin (xiaowenbo, not elevated). Deleting C:\\Windows.old requires admin privileges via ACL. Danger-full-access in DSH refers to the file sandbox of the harness; but does pwsh run elevated? Earlier WindowsIdentity said non-admin. Deleting Windows.old will hit Access Denied unless the folder ACL grants the user or the sandbox bypasses ACL (it shouldn't). We might need takeown/icacls which need admin. If truly non-elevated, Windows.old deletion may fail. Hmm. But wait — maybe the harness runs pwsh under an elevated service token? The WindowsIdentity check said Administrators role = False, so no.\n\nHmm, but that seems to make the biggest win impossible... unless DSH's danger-full-access uses a privileged file backend that performs operations as a different (privileged) process. The pwsh tool description says commands may run under a file sandbox; with danger-full-access \"The DSH file sandbox does not restrict file modifications by available operations.\" It's still the same pwsh token presumably.\n\nWe'll find out by attempting. But note approval policy \"never\": sandbox denials are final and I must not set sandbox_permissions. So if a deletion is ACL-denied, no escalation possible — explain to user to run as admin.\n\nLet me proceed with analysis. Steps:\n- collect pwsh-2\n- verify Windows.old & list top\n- AppData\\Local breakdown (background, could be slow; limit with shallow top-level dirs only: measure each direct child of AppData\\Local separately — but recursion of 50GB may take a while. Use background job.)\n\nLet me also check hibernation config."}}},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934746367,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":1,"block":{"type":"text","text":"权限已放开（full access）。现在把分析做完：确认 Windows.old 真