{"type":"assistant/message","seq":99,"time":1788934762762,"data":{"turn":2,"step":5,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"reasoning","text":"Very interesting: Under danger-full-access, `C:\\Windows.old` returns *nothing* for Get-ChildItem and Get-Item. Yet earlier (workspace-write mode), the root listing showed Windows.old and recursive measure returned 43,637.7 MB, and hiberfil.sys showed 22,799.3 MB.\n\nWait — that earlier listing might have been affected by the sandbox, but which direction? Let me think about what's going on:\n\nOption A: Earlier, in workspace-write sandbox, DSH virtualized/hallucinated? No — DSH wouldn't fabricate. But actually the sandbox could *redirect* C:\\ paths to a sandboxed overlay? Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Look at the first command's temp var earlier: `C:\\Users\\XIAOWE~1\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\dsh-vl9AWx` — 8.3 short name. In the very first Get-PSDrive result all drives showed 0 Used and 0 Free — clearly the sandboxed pwsh was in a mode where volume queries returned zeros (simulated?). Then DriveInfo gave real numbers.\n\nThen measurements: C:\\ root listing in workspace-write mode showed Windows.old (43.6GB measured) and hiberfil.sys 22.3GB. Now under danger-full-access, Windows.old appears nonexistent?! That's backwards from expectation. Unless... the current machine truly has no Windows.old and no hiberfil.sys, and the earlier numbers came from a *different* filesystem view (e.g., commands earlier ran against a sandbox profile or a different host...).\n\nHmm, wait. Danger-full-access \"DSH file sandbox does not restrict file modifications.\" Earlier \"workspace-write: may modify files under session workspace + some platform temporary areas.\" Filesystem *reads*: in workspace-write, reading anywhere probably allowed. Now danger-full-access — reading anywhere also allowed.\n\nWhy would Windows.old disappear? Possibility: These pwsh calls run in *separate containers/agents* each with possibly different filesystem redirection? Unlikely.\n\nAnother thought: maybe C:\\ root listing earlier wasn't the real machine C: — the harness pwsh may run inside a job object with its own view. Honestly, I think something's off between environments. Possibly the earlier measurement was against a *sandbox overlay* where C:\\Windows.old was copied/visible? No...\n\nActually — reconsider: The earlier command `$targets` measured Windows.old = 43,637.7 MB and hiberfil '不存在'. But root listing showed hiberfil.sys = 22,799.3 MB and Windows.old folder. Then Get-Item hiberfil.sys? Never tested. Now full-access Get-Item Windows.old → not found, Get-ChildItem Windows.old → empty, but hiberfil.sys earlier had a size 22799.3 via Length in listing.\n\nCould it be that the C:\\ listing *was* real (hiberfil.sys 22.8GB real, Windows.old real 43.6GB) but now under danger-full-access... the harness may *drop* processes into a restricted... no, that makes no sense either.\n\nWait, maybe I misread: perhaps now commands run in ConstrainedLanguage or a *different* user context? Let me check: what does C:\\ list now under full access? And check where Windows.old went, plus re-list root with sizes. Also re-measure hiberfil.sys existence.\n\nAlso the system: Windows 10 Pro 25H2, build 26200 — interesting: 26200 is actually Windows 11 25H2 build numbering (Windows 10 25H2 doesn't exist publicly; build 26200 = Win11 25H2). ProductName says Windows 10 Pro but build 26200 is Win11 25H2. Whatever.\n\nLet me directly re-run the root listing now and also test writing ability."},{"type":"text","text":"奇怪：放开权限后反而读不到 Windows.old 了。需要弄清楚当前文件系统视图到底如何。重新全面检查 C 盘根目录："},{"type":"tool-call","id":"call_00_hI1yMxvdo3ULmZ3j7xcQ4137","name":"pwsh","arguments":"{\"command\": \"Get-ChildItem C:\\\\ -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object Name, @{N='Type';E={if($_.PSIsContainer){'目录'}else{'文件'}}}, @{N='SizeMB';E={if(-not $_.PSIsContainer){[math]::Round($_.Length/1MB,1)}}}, LastWriteTime | Format-Table -AutoSize\", \"description\": \"Re-lis