ild","Item"," \\\"$","env",":L","OC","AL","AP","PD","ATA","\\\\","Cr","ash","D","umps","\\\""," -","Force"," -","File"," -","Error","Action"," Sil","ently","Continue"," |"," Select","-","Object"," -","First"," ","5"," Name",","," Last","Write","Time"," |"," Format","-","Table"," -","Auto","Size","\"",", ","\"","description","\"",": ","\"","In","spect"," internal"," structure"," of"," cache"," folders","\"","}"]},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934850620,"chunk":{"type":"block-start","index":3,"blockType":"tool-call"}},{"type":"tool-call-chunks","time0":1788934850620,"index":3,"dt":[16,0,0,21,0,0,0,23,0,0,0,0,22,0,0,0,0,23,0,0,0,1,0,16,1,22,0,0,0,0,0,22,0,0,0,31,0,0,0,0,0,13,0,0,0,24,0,0,37,10,50,0,15,0,0,0,1,0,6,0,0,0,1,0,34,0,0,0,0,1,59,0,0,17,0,0,20,0,0,0,7,0,18,0,40,0,0,36],"id":"call_01_QBZIPRsM7WLawbGtISIa9957","name":"pwsh","args":["","{","\"","command","\"",": ","\"","'","---"," HP"," ","打印机","是否","已","安装"," ---","'\\n","try"," {"," Get","-Pr","inter"," -","Error","Action"," Stop"," |"," Select","-","Object"," Name",","," Driver","Name"," |"," Format","-","Table"," -","Auto","Size"," }"," catch"," {"," '","查询","打印机","失败","(","需","权限","?)","'"," }\\n","'","---"," ","休眠","配置"," ---","'\\n","power","cfg"," /","a"," ","2",">&","1"," |"," Out","-","String","\"",", ","\"","description","\"",": ","\"","Check"," installed"," printers"," and"," h","ibernate"," config","\"","}"]},{"type":"chunk","time":1788934851230,"chunk":{"type":"block-end","index":0,"block":{"type":"reasoning","text":"AppData\\Local breakdown (top 25):\n- Yarn: 14,186.6 MB (~13.9 GB) — Yarn global cache! In AppData\\Local\\Yarn\\Cache typically. Safe to clear? Yarn cache can be regenerated. Yes, deleting yarn cache is safe (just slower installs later). But wait, is this really \"Yarn\"? Might contain offline mirror. Deleting Yarn cache = safe-ish but maybe user has projects needing offline installs. Default action: these caches are regenerable. It's 14GB! Actually AppData\\Local\\Yarn\\Cache holds package tarballs. Clearing frees 14GB but next yarn install re-downloads. Reasonable cleanup candidate — but I should confirm with user since some might keep it for offline. Hmm. Since caches are regenerable, cleaning them is the standard \"clean C drive\" action. But caution: they may rely on offline? Probably not. I'll list them and ask.\n- JetBrains: 9,184.9 MB (~9 GB) — JetBrains Toolbox caches? AppData\\Local\\JetBrains contains Toolbox + per-IDE caches (index caches, etc.). Some parts safe to clean (caches), some are IDE installs via Toolbox? Toolbox installs IDEs under AppData\\Local\\Programs (not JetBrains folder). AppData\\Local\\JetBrains has per-version folders with log/caches. Can be cleaned partially but risky to blanket-delete. Moderate.\n- pip: 4,375.7 MB (~4.3 GB) — pip cache (AppData\\Local\\pip\\cache). Safe to clear: `pip cache purge`. Regenerable.\n- wsl: 3,462 MB — WSL? AppData\\Local\\wsl contains... actually WSL distro vhdx usually in AppData\\Local\\Packages\\...\\LocalState. \"wsl\" folder 3.4GB might be the wsl disk? Can't blanket delete!\n- Kingsoft: 3,274.8 MB — WPS Office cache/data. Cleanable partially; includes backup files. Risky to blanket delete; but Kingsoft cache folder ~3GB... \n- Microsoft: 2,763.7 MB — includes Edge caches, Windows caches etc; not blanket-cleanable.\n- Google: 2,694.2 MB — Chrome profile (cache already counted). Not blanket delete.\n- Programs: 2,295 MB — installed per-user apps (e.g., VSCode, etc.) — don't delete!\n- Temp: 2,140.8 MB — clean!\n- npm-cache: 1,911.5 MB — safe to clean (npm cache clean --force / npm cache verify). Regenerable.\n- OpenAI: 699 MB — codex? don't delete.\n- pnpm-cache: 642 MB — pnpm store; can prune but pnpm store is a hard-link store! Deleting breaks node_modules hard links. pnpm store prune is safe-ish. Better to leave or use pnpm store prune. Actually pnpm store serves all projects; removing frees space but requires re-fetch; hardlinks keep working. pnpm store prune removes unreferenced packages. Recommend prune not delete.\n- Packages: 563.9 MB (UWP apps local state)\n- @genieworkbuddy-desktop-updater: 488 MB — app updater cache\n- 微信开发者工具: 461.9 MB — WeChat DevTools cache\n- trae-cli: 408 MB\n- Everything: 292.5 MB — Everything search index (don't delete while running; it's the index DB. Deleting means reindex. Could be okay but modest.)\n- CrashDumps: 121 MB — safe to clean.\n- updater folders (draw.io 275MB, apifox 193MB, @attuelectron 102MB, attu 86MB, mqttx 68MB) — old updater installers; typically safe to clean but small-ish; total ~700MB.\n- node-gyp: 51MB.\n\nHmm wait: Yarn 14GB is huge — likely Yarn offline cache from a monorepo? Or the \"Yarn\" folder could include `Yarn\\Cache` with many tarballs. Let me inspect the top few big folders' internals to classify before recommending. But enumerating 14GB recursive again is slow-ish but okay; only need one or two levels deep.\n\nAlso C:\\M1130MFP_M1210MFP_Full_Solution is clearly the HP LaserJet M1130/M1210 full driver package (installer leftovers) — 545MB. Deleting requires admin (root ACL)? We can test by attempting deletion of the folder — but approval never + danger-full-access file policy means the harness sandbox won't block; only Windows ACL will. Try `Remove-Item -Recurse`; if access denied, note it. Actually careful: don't test-delete something we shouldn't. The folder is a leftover installer that was unzipped to C:\\ root — safe to remove if the printer is installed. Removing it only deletes the installer payload. Let me check if there's an HP printer currently installed (Get-Printer) to confirm. If printer drivers installed, deletion is safe. Test ACL with Remove-Item attempt; on Access Denied we skip (would need admin).\n\nAlso should check: does user still use Hibernate? hiberfil 22.8GB. Disabling hibernation via powercfg needs admin. Since no admin & no approval prompts — can't do it. I can still *try* `powercfg /h off` to see if it works (maybe user token can?). It will almost surely fail without elevation. Actually powercfg /h off requires admin. We can check `powercfg /a` (available sleep states) without admin to see if hibernate is enabled.\n\nGiven the mess (some big targets need admin which we don't have, and user can't approve), the practical plan:\n\nCleanable now (no admin needed):\n1. %TEMP% user temp: 2.1GB — delete contents.\n2. pip cache 4.3GB — `pip cache purge` (or delete folder).\n3. npm cache 1.9GB — `npm cache clean --force` (admin not needed). Actually npm cache clean --force requires nothing special. Yes.\n4. Yarn cache 14GB — yarn cache clean (Yarn v1: `yarn cache clean`). Yarn 2+/Berry uses different. Check folder structure: AppData\\Local\\Yarn\\Cache → Yarn v1 cache (npm-style .zip tarballs in subfolders). Yarn v1 `yarn cache clean` clears it. Deleting folder contents manually is equally fine (regenerable). 14GB win!\n5. Chrome cache 308MB + Edge cache 353MB (browser closed → safe). Also INetCache.\n6. CrashDumps 121MB.\n7. pnpm store prune 642MB partial. Or delete entire pnpm-cache? pnpm store content is content-addressed; if we delete while node_modules exist, hardlinks keep data but store gone. pnpm store prune removes only unreferenced. Given complexity & modest gain, do `pnpm store prune`? Need pnpm on PATH. Simpler: skip pnpm or clear only cache subfolder.\n8. Updater leftover folders (draw.io 275, apifox 193, etc.): those updater dirs hold pending update packages; deleting is generally fine but app may complain/redo. ~700MB total. Could include. Maybe do these individually only if sizes confirmed; risk low-ish. Let me include with caution or skip. Since no approval possible, maybe keep to *clearly safe, regenerable* stuff: temp, pip, npm, yarn, browser caches, crashdumps.\n9. ProgramData package caches? skip.\n\nAdmin-needed (report, can't perform): hiberfil 22.3GB (powercfg /h off — user runs as admin), $SysReset 181MB, C:\\M1130MFP 545MB (folder deletion ACL at root likely admin; though maybe the user created it? LastWrite 2026/6/4. Try Remove-Item, fallback: report), DISM component cleanup, WinSxS.\n\nBefore deleting Yarn 14GB etc., confirm these are caches not something else. Let me inspect top-level of Yarn, JetBrains, pip, Kingsoft folders (one level) to classify, and check installed HP printer, plus powercfg /a state.\n\nActually — big decision: 14GB Yarn cache deletion is significant and affects future offline installs (dev machine likely online). I think asking the user a compact question with recommended defaults is appropriate given irreversibility, even though approvals are disabled (ask_user_question is chat-level, not approval). The user already said twice \"帮我清理下C盘\" and granted full access & never approvals — implying \"just do it\". But deleting dev caches (yarn/npm/pip) vs. personal files is a judgment call. Regenerable caches are conventionally safe. I'd proceed with the standard safe list, but let me ask ONE consolidated question about the borderline items (Yarn/npm/pip/pnpm caches, browser caches, temp, crash dumps, updater leftovers) so the user owns irreversible choices. Options: \"全清(推荐)\" vs custom vs skip caches. That's reasonable — not overly chatty, single question, and respects \"never approval\" (it's not an approval prompt, it's scope confirmation). Hmm, but the user might get annoyed: they asked twice already to just clean. The system has approval disabled; full access granted. The user's latest message: \"帮我清理下c盘空间\" repeated after I was mid-analysis. That suggests they want action NOW.\n\nCompromise: perform the unambiguous, regenerable, zero-risk cleanups immediately (temp files, browser caches, crash dumps, pip/npm/yarn caches are all regenerable caches — standard disk-cleanup targets), verify space freed. Hold off only on things that aren't pure caches or that need admin, and report them with instructions. That best matches intent. Risk check:\n- pip cache purge: fine.\n- npm cache clean --force: fine.\n- Yarn v1 cache clean: fine (regenerable; installs just re-download).\n- Browser cache delete: fine.\n- %TEMP% delete: fine (skip files in use).\n- CrashDumps delete: fine.\n- JetBrains caches: these are IDE *system* caches/indexes; deleting full JetBrains folder could nuke IDE configs? AppData\\Local\\JetBrains contains both per-IDE \"system\" folders and Toolbox. Deleting whole = IDE reindex (annoying) and could include local history! Not purely cache. SKIP blanket; too risky without user confirmation.\n- Kingsoft (WPS): contains user caches but also maybe important data. SKIP.\n- wsl 3.4GB: skip, could be disk images.\n- Microsoft/Google/Programs: skip.\n\nSo auto-clean: TEMP (2.1GB) + Chrome (308MB) + Edge (353MB) + INetCache + CrashDumps (121MB) + pip (4.3GB) + npm (1.9GB) + Yarn (14GB) ≈ 23GB. That gets C: from 49.4GB to ~72GB free. Nice.\n\nPlus try: M1130 folder (545MB) if ACL allows — check printer presence first; it's an installer not needed. Actually safe regardless of printer (payload can be re-downloaded; driver stays installed). If deletion denied → report admin step.\n\nWait — before wiping Yarn 14GB blindly, verify it's the cache folder (AppData\\Local\\Yarn\\Cache\\...). If the Yarn folder instead contains something else (like a global install dir), deleting is wrong. Inspect first (one level). Same for verifying pip is the cache folder not site-packages (AppData\\Local\\pip is the cache location, yes; global user site is AppData\\Roaming\\Python or Local\\Programs\\Python). Confirm internal structure quickly.\n\nAlso confirm yarn cache clean command availability; simpler: remove AppData\\Local\\Yarn\\Cache contents directly (that IS the v1 cache path). Also possibly \"Yarn\\Cache\\v6\" etc.\n\nLet me run a quick inspection then perform cleanup in one go with careful logging, and check powercfg + printer. Sequence:\n1. Inspect Yarn/pip/npm internal layout (fast, top dirs), printer presence, powercfg /a.\n2. Perform deletions with a generated report: measure before/after free 