folders: `npm-vue-antd-jeecg-3.1.0-...` (~2.9GB + ~2.0GB). These are Yarn offline cache entries for a **vue-antd-jeecg** project (a JeecgBoot frontend template) — nearly 5GB! It looks like a Yarn v1 \"offline mirror\" cache with huge tarballs — possibly the user's project vendors entire node_modules into yarn cache. This appears to be an offline mirror being actively used by many running node processes (35+ node processes!). Killing these processes would be disruptive — they're likely the user's dev servers/IDEs.\n\nWait — but interesting: two identical-ish entries (~2.9GB and ~2.0GB) named npm-vue-antd-jeecg-3.1.0-<uuid>-<timestamp>. These look like Yarn offline mirror artifacts... 2.9GB for one npm package is enormous — it's probably a full vendor dump. These may be referenced by an active build.\n\nShould I force-delete? It's cache; but with 35 running node processes tied to it, deleting could break a running build (though yarn cache deletion normally only affects future installs, files in use may keep running). Since the files are locked/in-use, deletion keeps failing. The right call: don't kill the user's processes. Report that 4.8GB remains in Yarn cache, locked by active node processes; it will be clearable when dev processes are closed, or re-run later.\n\nHmm, but wait: could try deleting again now since error was transient (\"Could not find a part of the path\" = a race while something writes). Given two giant folders, maybe a build is actively writing. Attempting another round risks nothing (delete fails gracefully). But repeated hammering is pointless if locked. Let me try once more quickly; if still stuck, finalize with report.\n\nAlso update todo list: mark HP folder done, cleanup done except Yarn residue.\n\nAlso: measure final free space on C: again for the summary, and maybe also verify each target state. Then write summary with:\n- Freed ~21 GB (Temp 2.1, pip 4.3, npm 1.9, browsers 0.66, CrashDumps 0.12, HP 0.55, plus earlier partial Yarn... wait Y